WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.521
A working controller still needs suitable
parameters.

00:00:03.521 --> 00:00:07.721
An aggressive response can overshoot,
while a conservative response may settle

00:00:07.721 --> 00:00:08.351
slowly.

00:00:08.351 --> 00:00:12.170
This project explores reinforcement
learning to tune the adaptive reference

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:15.988
dynamics before deployment, balancing
tracking behavior and control effort for

00:00:15.988 --> 00:00:17.000
underwater maneuvers.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.958
The control architecture contains adaptive
reference management followed by

00:00:21.958 --> 00:00:23.320
model predictive control.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.052
Reinforcement learning acts on selected
tuning parameters of the adaptive

00:00:27.052 --> 00:00:27.668
layer.

00:00:27.668 --> 00:00:32.519
It does not replace the predictive
controller, and it does not issue new

00:00:32.519 --> 00:00:35.000
actuator commands at every control step.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.792
The tuning variables are the natural
frequencies of the desired depth and

00:00:40.792 --> 00:00:42.067
heading reference dynamics.

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:44.739
Changing them shapes the requested
transient response.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:48.931
Their effects must be evaluated in the
complete closed loop, because faster

00:00:48.931 --> 00:00:52.424
desired dynamics do not automatically
produce less overshoot or better

00:00:52.424 --> 00:00:53.000
performance.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.937
Before each simulation episode, the agent
chooses a candidate pair of

00:00:58.937 --> 00:00:59.587
frequencies.

00:00:59.587 --> 00:01:03.786
The simulation is reset and a complete
maneuver is evaluated.

00:01:03.786 --> 00:01:07.196
That parameter pair stays fixed throughout
the episode.

00:01:07.196 --> 00:01:11.000
The trial response then informs the next
offline choice.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.332
Each response is assessed for tracking
error, overshoot, settling behavior,

00:01:16.332 --> 00:01:17.694
and control effort.

00:01:17.694 --> 00:01:21.798
A useful candidate balances these
objectives rather than improving one by

00:01:21.798 --> 00:01:23.160
making another unacceptable.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.519
The illustrative comparison shows why the
fastest-looking initial response

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:29.000
need not be the preferred tuning.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.442
Offline learning searches for a useful
parameter set within the considered

00:01:34.442 --> 00:01:35.019
domain.

00:01:35.019 --> 00:01:38.512
A selected candidate then needs validation
on representative commands and

00:01:38.512 --> 00:01:39.088
conditions.

00:01:39.088 --> 00:01:43.978
The aim is better tuning for those cases,
not proof of a globally ideal

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:47.000
setting or a universal guarantee of zero
overshoot.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.243
The selected frequencies are loaded into
the adaptive reference layer.

00:01:52.243 --> 00:01:55.663
During operation, those tuning values
remain fixed while the controller

00:01:55.663 --> 00:01:58.279
responds to new depth and heading
commands.

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:01.699
Adaptive compensation can still evolve
with measurements; fixed tuning does

00:02:01.699 --> 00:02:05.000
not mean that the adaptive layer is
switched off.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:11.240
The final comparison should use the
original and learned tuning under the same

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.452
conditions, measuring overshoot, settling
time, tracking error, and control

00:02:14.452 --> 00:02:15.041
effort.

00:02:15.041 --> 00:02:17.057
This preview explains that workflow.

00:02:17.057 --> 00:02:20.627
It does not present trained-policy results
or measured improvement, because

00:02:20.627 --> 00:02:23.000
the research is still in development.
